{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PADDk8jT9zBPFeavTHXowW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Output: \"hello world\\n foo\\n\" — there's a leading space before \"foo\". The expected is \"hello world\\nfoo\\n\" without a space. So my hard-break at exact boundary is correct but the remaining text starts with the space.\n\nThe issue is that GNU fold with -s likely consumes the space that falls right at/after the boundary. Let me rethink the algorithm.\n\nLooking at GNU coreutils source for fold, the -s behavior: when breaking a line, if the character after the break position is a space, it moves past the space(s). Or alternatively: the break includes the space, and R3.3 says the space is the last char of the current line. But if the space is past the width, it can't be on the current line...\n\nI think the correct GNU behavior for -s is: after finding the break point and writing the line, skip any spaces that immediately follow the break point before starting the next segment. Let me try that approach.\n\nActually, let me re-read R3.3 very carefully: \"The space character at the break point is written as the last character of the current output line before the inserted newline.\"\n\nSo when -s triggers a break at a space, the space IS written to the current line. For \"hello world foo\\n\" with width 11:\n- The first 11 columns are \"hello world\" (no break needed yet, col == width exactly)\n- The next char at position 11 is ' '. With my current code (hard break at boundary), position 11 starts the next line.\n- But perhaps GNU fold, with -s, sees that position 11 is a space and includes it in the break — writing the space as the last character of current line and the next line starts at position 12.\n\nBut that would make the current line 12 columns wide (\"hello world \") which exceeds width 11. Unless the space doesn't count as exceeding...\n\nOR maybe GNU fold with -s doesn't break at the exact boundary but instead continues scanning until it must break (i.e., the column would exceed width), and only then looks for the last space. With input \"hello world foo\\n\" and width 11:\n- Scan through \"hello world\" = 11 columns (position 10). Col == width, but with -s, we continue because the next character might be a space or word boundary.\n- Position 11 is ' '. Since col would become 12 > 11, we need to break. With -s, find the last space at or before position 11. That's position 11 itself! Break after position 11 (the space). Per R3.3, the space is the last char of the current line. But wait, outputting \"hello world \" is 12 columns...\n\nI think I need to look at this differently. Maybe GNU fold with -s considers the space to not take up a column for breaking purposes. Or more likely, when the character that exceeds the width IS a space, it's consumed as part of the break.\n\nLet me try a different approach: in the `newCol > width` case, if the current character is a space and -s is active, consume it and break."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2045,"cache_read_input_tokens":80157,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2045},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab279970-93cc-4d72-a577-0214f24f20cf","uuid":"553b6a56-1299-4690-a99f-985acbadbbf3"}
