{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JWj16BvZZLjfkFT9FHAHrs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the output is \"hello world \\nfoo\\n\" — there's a trailing space on the first line. The expected is \"hello world\\nfoo\\n\" without a trailing space. So the space is consumed but should NOT be written to the current line.\n\nThe issue is that I'm returning `i+2` (or `width+1`) which means positions 0 through i+1 are written, including the space. I need to break after the boundary character but skip the space. \n\nHmm, this is tricky because the `foldLine` function writes `content[:breakPos]`. If `breakPos` includes the space, it writes the space. I need to either return the break position before the space and then skip the space in the remaining, or handle it differently.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the approach should be: return `i+1` (break at the boundary, not including the space), then in the caller, skip the space. But the caller doesn't know about -s.\n\nActually, looking at how GNU fold works, I think the approach is: when at the exact boundary with -s and the next char is a space, return `i+1` (the hard boundary), and separately handle skipping the space in the caller. But the current architecture doesn't support that.\n\nLet me redesign: return two values from findBreakPos — the break position (what to write) and the skip position (where to resume). Normally they're the same, but with -s consuming a space, skip = break + 1.\n\nActually, a simpler approach: instead of returning the position of what to include, return the position where the next segment starts. Then let the write be `content[:writeEnd]` and the next segment be `content[nextStart:]`. Let me refactor.\n\nActually, the simplest fix: change the meaning of the return value to \"start of next segment\" and handle the space-skip there. Then in foldLine, write `content[:breakPos]` excluding the trailing space when needed... no, that's getting complicated.\n\nLet me just change the return to be two values: end of current segment and start of next segment."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":271,"cache_read_input_tokens":85134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":271},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab279970-93cc-4d72-a577-0214f24f20cf","uuid":"fe478a49-355b-4e06-aef1-a1d3198b6eb7"}
